-- Literal percent signs in `@nlg` prose.
--
-- Inside an `@nlg` annotation, `%` delimits a natural-language reference, so
-- `%amount%` renders that parameter. The delimiters are tight: `% word %` is
-- prose, not a reference.
--
-- Without that tightness the `%` of a written-out percentage pairs with the `%`
-- opening the next real reference, capturing the word between them as an
-- identifier and demoting the intended reference to plain text.
-- Regression test for smucclaw/l4-ide#957.

-- One word between the two percent signs, not in scope. This used to report
-- "could not find a definition for the identifier `with`".
GIVEN amount IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
@nlg 5% with %amount%
DECIDE `over threshold` IF amount > 100

#CHECK `over threshold` WITH amount IS 200

-- The same shape, but the captured word is a parameter in scope. This used to
-- typecheck clean and quietly render `rate` where the author wrote a percent sign,
-- with the intended `%amount%` reduced to literal text.
GIVEN rate IS A NUMBER
      amount IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
@nlg a 5% rate %amount%
DECIDE `scaled amount` IS amount TIMES rate

#EVAL `scaled amount` 2 50

-- References themselves are unaffected: tight, and still resolved.
GIVEN amount IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
@nlg %amount% doubled
DECIDE doubled IS amount TIMES 2

#EVAL doubled 21

-- Tightness keys on WHITESPACE, so a percent pair with none still captures:
-- `10%and%20` binds `and`. A backslash escapes what tightness cannot reach.
GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
@nlg a rate between 10\%and\%20 applied to %n%
DECIDE `the levy on` IS n TIMES 0.1

#EVAL `the levy on` 100

-- The close bracket needs the same escape, for a different reason and with a
-- different failure. `[...]` is an inline annotation, so a literal `]` ends it
-- early and the remainder hits the enclosing parser as `unexpected ]`. That one
-- is loud; the percent above is silent, which is why both are tested but only
-- this one could ever have been caught by a golden.
--
-- This one and `note on` below are written INSIDE the head, after the input, so
-- until smucclaw/l4-ide#972 was fixed the `.nlg.golden` beside this file showed
-- their rules' bare names while `l4 render` showed the sentences. They now head
-- their calls in both projections, which is what makes the escapes visible to a
-- golden at all -- see `ok/nlg-head-placement.l4` for the whole placement table.
GIVEN m IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the duty on` m [rate \] per unit, see s.3 \% of value] MEANS m TIMES 0.2

#EVAL `the duty on` 50

-- A backslash that cannot BEGIN an escape stays ordinary text, exactly as it
-- was before escapes existed. The lexer consumes only the escapes the decoder
-- honours (`\%`, `\]`, `\\`), so `\q` is two ordinary characters; and a line
-- annotation has no closing herald, so a trailing `\` has nothing to escape.
-- Both of these lexed before this feature and must still lex. The exactprint
-- golden pins that they re-emit unchanged.
GIVEN q IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
@nlg a discount of 50\
DECIDE discounted IS q TIMES 0.5

#EVAL discounted 80

GIVEN r IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`note on` r [see s.3\q here] MEANS r TIMES 1

#EVAL `note on` 7
